WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.421 --> 00:00:16.628
Hello! Welcome Superstars in History!

2
00:00:17.689 --> 00:00:25.254
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and you're in for a royal treat today— another exclusive interview with a

3
00:00:25.414 --> 00:00:27.636
famous person from the past.

4
00:00:28.576 --> 00:00:33.780
Today's guest was a caring king who worked hard to improve life in his kingdom.

5
00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:41.424
In fact, he was so devoted to his people, he invented a brand-new alphabet for them.

6
00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:48.651
Straight from history, here is His Majesty King Sejong the Great!

7
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.382
It pleases me to be here, Faye.

8
00:01:07.723 --> 00:01:10.365
The honor is all mine, Your Majesty.

9
00:01:10.385 --> 00:01:17.372
I'm so excited to interview the most beloved king from the Joseon Dynasty of Korea.

10
00:01:18.753 --> 00:01:24.198
But I have to say, the surprising thing is that you became a king at all, since you

11
00:01:24.218 --> 00:01:27.901
were the third son of your father, King Taejong.

12
00:01:28.662 --> 00:01:30.624
How did you end up on the throne?

13
00:01:31.912 --> 00:01:36.354
As a child I was studious and serious, and my father looked kindly upon this.

14
00:01:37.495 --> 00:01:43.098
Meanwhile my oldest brother, Prince Yangnyeong, who was the heir to the throne, was carefree and lacked the

15
00:01:43.158 --> 00:01:46.980
temperament required of a king.

16
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:53.083
In 1418 the court officials were so exasperated by his behavior, they appealed to my father, saying the

17
00:01:53.143 --> 00:01:54.604
prince was unfit to rule.

18
00:01:55.825 --> 00:02:00.267
At the urging of these officials, my father stripped Prince Yangnyeong of the title crown prince and named

19
00:02:00.347 --> 00:02:01.448
me the heir instead.

20
00:02:02.508 --> 00:02:05.909
Two months later my father abdicated, and the throne passed to me.

21
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.650
You were only 21 when you became king.

22
00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.291
That must have been a huge responsibility!

23
00:02:15.312 --> 00:02:17.352
It was, and it happened very suddenly.

24
00:02:18.373 --> 00:02:22.214
I did not feel ready to become king, but my father gave me no choice.

25
00:02:23.654 --> 00:02:28.716
After his abdication he stayed involved in state affairs and daily governance for a few years to make

26
00:02:28.756 --> 00:02:29.736
the transition smooth.

27
00:02:31.028 --> 00:02:35.809
In 1422 he fell ill and died, and from then on, I ruled alone.

28
00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.951
Can you tell us about some of your accomplishments as king?

29
00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:41.751
Certainly, Faye.

30
00:02:42.271 --> 00:02:46.673
I'm sure you want to know about my alphabet, but even before I invented it, I had a

31
00:02:46.713 --> 00:02:47.853
deep interest in learning.

32
00:02:48.853 --> 00:02:52.234
As a child I loved to read and often read all through the night!

33
00:02:53.535 --> 00:02:57.336
To promote learning I established the Hall of Worthies early in my reign.

34
00:02:58.272 --> 00:03:03.276
This was a group of scholars whom I chose to advise me on policy, and research ways to

35
00:03:03.336 --> 00:03:05.077
improve the lives of my people.

36
00:03:06.378 --> 00:03:11.842
I recruited scholars from all backgrounds since their abilities were more important to me than their social class.

37
00:03:13.063 --> 00:03:19.148
Your reign saw advances in science and technology—a lot like the Renaissance!

38
00:03:20.369 --> 00:03:24.392
I do not know what "the Renaissance" is, young lady, but you are correct.

39
00:03:25.444 --> 00:03:29.326
I believed that science and technology would help to strengthen our nation.

40
00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:34.928
A talented inventor named Jang Yeongsil invented a great many things at my request.

41
00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:39.591
He built water clocks, astrolabes, and sundials.

42
00:03:40.671 --> 00:03:45.333
He also made a rain gauge and a water gauge that allowed us to better manage our water

43
00:03:45.353 --> 00:03:51.036
supply. And he improved the metal printing press so that books could be printed even faster.

44
00:03:52.364 --> 00:03:57.408
Now that you've mentioned books, let's move on to your alphabet, Hangeul!

45
00:03:58.528 --> 00:04:00.009
Hangeul? No, no.

46
00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:05.873
The name for my writing system is Hunminjeongeum, which means "correct sounds for the instruction of people."

47
00:04:07.495 --> 00:04:13.279
Back in my time, our language had no writing system of its own—we wrote with Chinese characters.

48
00:04:14.139 --> 00:04:20.744
But Chinese characters did not represent our speech accurately because our language was very different from Chinese.

49
00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:28.719
And learning Chinese characters was challenging; only elite men had time for this kind of study.

50
00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:35.302
I was saddened by the fact that so many of my subjects were trapped in illiteracy with few

51
00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:37.443
opportunities to acquire new knowledge.

52
00:04:38.743 --> 00:04:43.826
With some help from my children, I invented a new writing system that would be easy for everyone

53
00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:44.266
to learn.

54
00:04:45.586 --> 00:04:47.867
My creation was released to the public in 1446.

55
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.061
Your alphabet is unique among the world's writing systems.

56
00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:57.702
The shapes of the letters come from the sounds they make.

57
00:04:58.902 --> 00:05:00.823
You are a very bright young lady!

58
00:05:01.443 --> 00:05:04.124
You would make an excellent addition to the Hall of Worthies.

59
00:05:05.824 --> 00:05:11.626
Indeed, the shape of each consonant is inspired by the shape or position of the speech organs when

60
00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:12.626
making its sound.

61
00:05:14.186 --> 00:05:20.488
For example, nieun, the consonant for n, is shaped like the tongue when making a n sound.

62
00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:27.413
And mieum, which is for m, is meant to look like the lips when making a m sound.

63
00:05:29.435 --> 00:05:33.618
I bet everyone was thrilled to have such an easy-to-learn alphabet!

64
00:05:34.558 --> 00:05:40.643
Hardly! Many nobles and elites at court enjoyed the power of being literate while the common people were

65
00:05:40.683 --> 00:05:44.666
not. So my invention was met with much opposition.

66
00:05:45.706 --> 00:05:50.818
I tirelessly promoted the alphabet, but I'm sorry to report it was slow to spread.

67
00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.924
Literacy was only one of the areas where I tried to improve the lives of my people.

68
00:05:58.025 --> 00:06:00.788
I made the tax system fairer for the common people.

69
00:06:02.109 --> 00:06:07.313
I had a textbook on farming published to help farmers succeed in producing food for the kingdom.

70
00:06:08.515 --> 00:06:14.300
I even enacted laws to give government slave women maternity leave and their husbands paternity leave.

71
00:06:15.676 --> 00:06:18.357
It sounds like you were really ahead of your time.

72
00:06:19.338 --> 00:06:21.419
Some people think it's easy to be a king.

73
00:06:22.339 --> 00:06:24.180
But you worked really hard!

74
00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.061
It was a very hard job—and not a healthy one.

75
00:06:29.182 --> 00:06:35.004
As king, I was constantly reading documents, often in poor lighting, which weakened my eyesight.

76
00:06:36.125 --> 00:06:40.767
I was served delicious, rich food but had little opportunity for exercise.

77
00:06:42.388 --> 00:06:45.601
Needless to say, I wasn't always in the best of health.

78
00:06:46.582 --> 00:06:52.145
But as king, I felt a duty to my people, and I'm proud to have improved their lives

79
00:06:52.205 --> 00:06:52.785
where I could.

80
00:06:54.086 --> 00:06:59.489
With all that you accomplished, no one can accuse you of slacking off on the job!

81
00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.832
Thank you for sharing your story with us, Your Majesty.

82
00:07:04.652 --> 00:07:06.573
We'll let you get back to history now.

83
00:07:07.654 --> 00:07:18.417
Good-bye! King Sejong spent the last third of his life suffering from blindness and diabetes, and his son

84
00:07:18.457 --> 00:07:21.941
and heir, Prince Munjong, ruled as regent for him.

85
00:07:23.061 --> 00:07:28.386
King Sejong died in 1450, after 32 years on the throne.

86
00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:34.612
His reign is remembered for its advances in science, technology, and literacy.

87
00:07:35.848 --> 00:07:43.251
In fact, October 9 is a national holiday in Korea, Hangeul Day, celebrating the day that Hunminjeongeum was

88
00:07:43.351 --> 00:07:52.275
published. And King Sejong's alphabet is still so admired that one of UNESCO's international literacy prizes is named

89
00:07:52.395 --> 00:07:52.855
after him.

90
00:07:53.996 --> 00:07:55.757
I hope you enjoyed today's guest.

91
00:07:56.537 --> 00:08:02.940
Be sure to tune in again next time for another blast from the past on Superstars in History!
